      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Bariatric surgery leads to a rapid and sustained weight loss often accompanied with improvement in glucose homeostasis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The objective of this study was to investigate the effects of bariatric surgery on pancreatic lipid metabolism, blood flow, and glycemic control."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "This was a longitudinal study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "The study was conducted in a clinical research center."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "This study included 27 morbidly obese and 15 healthy control subjects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Measurements were performed using positron emission tomography with the palmitate analog 14(R,S)-[(18)F]fluoro-6-thia-heptadecanoic acid and radiowater ([(15)O]H2O) and computed tomography. In morbidly obese subjects, positron emission tomography/computed tomography imaging studies were performed before and 6 months after bariatric surgery (either Roux-en-Y gastric bypass or sleeve gastrectomy)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Pancreatic fat and fat-free volume, fatty acid uptake and blood flow were measured as well as parameters of β-cell function, glucose tolerance, and insulin sensitivity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Six months after bariatric surgery, 23% excess weight loss was observed (P < .0001), and diabetes remission was seen in 7 of 10 patients. When compared with preoperative values, after surgery, notable decreases in pancreatic fat volume (P < .01), fatty acid uptake, and blood flow (both P < .05) were seen, whereas no change was seen in pancreatic fat-free volume. The decrease in pancreatic fat volume and the preservation of blood flow were associated with favorable glucose homeostasis and β-cell function."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Bariatric surgery elicits marked alterations in pancreatic lipid metabolism and blood flow, which may contribute to the observed improvement in glucose homeostasis and remission of type 2 diabetes."
        }
      ]},
